.Dd October 28, 2026
.Dt SOFTWAREUPDATE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm softwareupdate
.Nd inspect and stage rmxOS boot-environment updates
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl -help
.Nm
.Fl -list Op Fl -catalog Ar directory-or-URL
.Nm
.Fl -history
.Nm
.Cm status
.Nm
.Fl -download Op Ar NAME Ns | Ns Cm all
.Nm
.Fl -install Op Fl -restart Op Fl -no-auto-confirm
.Op Ar NAME Ns | Ns Pa /path/to/update.tar
.Nm
.Cm confirm | cancel
.Nm
.Cm rollback Op Fl -restart
.Nm
.Cm verify Op Fl -quiet
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
provides update discovery, build inspection, and local archive installation
into a new boot environment.
Updates may be downloaded from a published catalog, then installed by name
from the verified incoming cache or from an absolute archive path.
The accepted filters
.Fl -recommended , -all , -os-only
select OS updates; every update is an OS update.
.Pp
.Fl -catalog
selects a local published directory, file:// URL, or http(s):// URL.
A URL may name the publication directory or its catalog.json.
Without that option, catalog_url is read from
.Pa /etc/defaults/softwareupdate.conf
and then overridden by
.Pa /etc/softwareupdate.conf .
The setting is a single catalog_url=value assignment, optionally double-quoted;
blank lines and full-line comments beginning with # are accepted.
No shell expansion is performed.
The shipped default is empty: no default publication host exists yet.
List and download then name catalog_url and exit nonzero until configured.
The directory contains
.Pa catalog.json
and one
.Pa rmxos-<commit12>/update.tar
with a
.Pa update.tar.sha256
sidecar per entry.
The sidecar is a lowercase 64-digit SHA256, optionally followed by whitespace.
Catalog reads use fetch(1). Local listing additionally checks regular archive
files, declared byte sizes and sidecar agreement; remote listing reads catalog
metadata only. Neither provides authentication of the publisher.
Signing is deferred.
.Sh DOWNLOAD AND NAMED INSTALL
.Fl -download
requires root. NAME downloads one catalog entry; all, or no operand, downloads
every entry. Each archive and sidecar is fetched with fetch(1) into a private
temporary directory under /var/db/softwareupdate/incoming.
The archive size and SHA256 must match the catalog, and its sidecar must name
the same SHA256. Only then is the directory renamed to its final update name.
A failed or cut transfer removes the temporary files and never gives a partial
file an installable name. A repeated verified download preserves existing files.
.Pp
.Fl -install Ar NAME
uses /var/db/softwareupdate/incoming/NAME/update.tar and its sidecar through the
same verification and clone-only engine as local archive installation.
It does not download implicitly. The install boot-environment preconditions
below still apply.
.Sh PUBLISHING
The build-host Mix project provides one compiled publisher command:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mix softwareupdate.publish OVERLAY PREVIOUS FROM_COMMIT COMMIT TREE
.Ed
OVERLAY and PREVIOUS are composed overlay directories with their manifests;
OVERLAY must also contain directories.tsv and its tree/files payloads.
The publisher verifies payload identities, packs update.tar with the existing
manifests, METALOG, commit, removals, report, directories.tsv and payload members,
and writes TREE/rmxos-COMMIT12/update.tar plus update.tar.sha256.
It appends a catalog entry whose from identity is FROM_COMMIT and the SHA256
of PREVIOUS/manifest.tsv. Existing update names refuse; other entries and their
archives are preserved. Catalog replacement is atomic and serialized by a
publication lock. The new entry becomes visible only after both archive files
are in place. This command does not sign updates or impose a downgrade floor.
.Sh CATALOG FORMAT
The top-level JSON object has exactly these fields:
.Bl -tag -width restart_required
.It format
The string
.Dq rmxos-catalog-v1 .
.It updates
An array of update objects.
Each has these required fields:
.Bl -tag -width manifest_sha256
.It name
.Dq rmxos-
followed by the first 12 lowercase hexadecimal digits of commit.
Names must be unique and cannot contain paths.
.It commit
The produced build's full 40-digit lowercase hexadecimal source commit.
.It sha256
The archive's 64-digit lowercase hexadecimal SHA256.
.It from
An object containing
.Cm commit
(the required installed build's 40-digit commit) and
.Cm manifest_sha256
(the SHA256 of its complete installed manifest bytes).
.It date
Publication date, a nonempty string, conventionally UTC ISO 8601.
.It size
The archive's byte length as an unsigned integer.
.It restart_required
A JSON boolean indicating whether the update needs a restart.
.El
.El
.Pp
An entry has
.Dq applies=yes
only if both
.Cm from.commit
and
.Cm from.manifest_sha256
match
.Pa /var/db/rmxos/commit
and the hash of
.Pa /var/db/rmxos/manifest.tsv .
Missing installed identity is reported as not applicable.
Unknown fields, duplicate names, invalid hashes and incomplete objects
are refused.
.Sh STATUS AND HISTORY
.Cm status
reports the mounted root dataset and BE name, actual SHA256 hashes of
.Pa /boot/RMXOS-RELEASE/kernel ,
.Pa /boot/RMXOS-RELEASE/mach.ko
and
.Pa /sbin/launchd ,
the recorded commit, staged target and confirmed BE, owner
.Pa /etc/*.rmxos-new
sidecars recursively, the
.Xr bectl 8
list, and the last ten history lines.
It does not use
.Xr uname 1
as build identity.
Missing image-install identity or state is printed as
.Dq none ;
unavailable files or BE information are explicitly marked unavailable.
Directory symlinks are not traversed.
Unreadable sidecar directories are named
as
.Dq sidecars_unreadable
and are not included in the sidecar count.
.Pp
State is read from
.Pa /var/db/rmxos/pending-target
and
.Pa /var/db/rmxos/confirmed-target .
.Fl -history
streams the complete
.Pa /var/db/softwareupdate/history.tsv
without changing it.
Status shows its last ten lines.
Each history line is tab-separated: UTC time, action, from-commit, to-commit,
previous BE, target BE, conflict count.
Missing history prints
.Dq history=none .
.Sh PRECONDITIONS
Download requires effective UID zero. Install and lifecycle commands additionally
require a running ZFS root
at exactly
.Pa <pool>/ROOT/<name> ,
a matching running entry in
.Cm bectl list -H ,
and no entry marked for a temporary boot, except cancellation of that selection.
Named install or download targets must differ from the running BE and
must not already exist.
For a local archive install, the target name is derived from the validated
.Pa ./commit
member.
An unspecified or
.Cm all
selection does not resolve a target in this version and stops before any
update engine runs.
All refusals start with
.Dq softwareupdate:
on standard error and exit nonzero.
.Sh LOCAL INSTALLATION
.Fl -install
with an absolute local archive path checks its SHA256 sidecar, the archive
member set, manifests, METALOG, payload hashes, modes and removal paths before
creating a boot environment.
Archives use the rmx-devup pack format: manifest.tsv, previous.tsv, METALOG,
commit, removals.json, report.json, optional baseline.tsv and directories.tsv,
and files/ payloads.
Only regular files and declared one-level internal hard links are supported.
Symlinks, traversal, duplicate members and extra payloads are refused.
Signatures are not implemented.
.Pp
The running manifest must equal previous.tsv or manifest.tsv.
A first update without installed manifest state requires baseline.tsv;
its /etc inventory is used only for configuration comparisons, never deletions.
Every destination is checked before cloning; the clone is checked again.
Symlink parents, nonregular destinations and crossings into other datasets
are refused.
.Pp
The command clones the running BE with
.Cm bectl create -e ,
mounts it, and writes only inside that clone.
Candidate files use a same-directory temporary file and rename, declared modes
and root/wheel ownership.
Unchanged previous-only files are removed; locally changed ones are kept.
Explicit obsolete files are removed even if edited.
Owner-modified /etc files stay and receive candidate .rmxos-new sidecars;
a differing existing sidecar refuses the update before cloning.
Files under /etc/mtree/ and /etc/defaults/ are system files and are always
replaced from the build, without producing sidecars.
Hard-link declarations are installed canonical-first and verified by inode
and exact link count.
.Pp
The optional directories.tsv starts with rmx-directories-v1 and contains
tab-separated octal mode, numeric UID, numeric GID and absolute directory path,
with a final newline. Modes have a leading zero and three or four octal digits;
UID and GID are unsigned decimal and must not be 4294967295.
Directory paths cannot contain empty, dot or dot-dot components.
The root path is permitted; other components use letters, digits, underscore,
dot, plus, at-sign and hyphen.
The directory manifest is covered by the archive checksum.
Missing directories are created in parent-first order in the clone with the
declared ownership and mode. Existing directories are never changed or removed.
Symlink and non-directory destinations and crossings into other datasets refuse
the update. Directory declarations cannot collide with file or removal paths.
The table is saved as /var/db/rmxos/directories.tsv in the clone.
Archives without it declare no new directories.
.Pp
The clone's RELEASE module hints are refreshed with
.Xr kldxref 8 .
Failure prevents activation and retains the clone for inspection.
Build state is written under /var/db/rmxos, and the owner's loader.conf.local
lines are preserved before appending the clone's root dataset selection.
Candidate bytes, modes, ownership and removals are verified.
The derived linker.hints and appended loader.conf.local are checked in their
generated form, rather than against their pre-generation archive hashes.
.Pp
The clone is unmounted, snapshotted as its root dataset's
.Cm @sealed-GUID ,
and held with the ZFS tag
.Cm softwareupdate .
Failure to snapshot or hold prevents activation.
The snapshot GUID is recorded as the environment's local ZFS user property
.Cm org.rmxos:seal-guid .
The GUID determines both its final name and its identity after promotion.
The sealed clone is selected for one boot with
.Cm bectl activate -t .
The command prints staged= and a reboot command.
.Fl -restart
runs shutdown only after successful staging.
The persistent running BE remains the fallback; this command never confirms.
.Fl -no-auto-confirm
is recorded in /var/db/softwareupdate/no-auto-confirm in the clone and disables
automatic confirmation; manual confirmation remains available.
History is appended on the running BE under /var/db/softwareupdate/history.tsv
and copied into the clone, with logger records tagged softwareupdate.
The running payload and its installed manifest are not modified.
Failures retain created clones, attempt to unmount them, and do not activate
them unless the failure happens after the activation command succeeded.
.Sh CONFIRMATION AND RECOVERY
Status reports the next boot environment selected with the T flag, together
with the staged commit recorded at installation, even before rebooting.
On the staged boot it reports the remaining confirmation window and whether
automatic confirmation is enabled.
.Pp
The launchd job org.rmxos.softwareupdate.boot-check runs once at boot.
When the running environment equals pending-target and automatic confirmation
is enabled, it waits until 600 seconds of monotonic uptime.
The three identity files must match their manifest hashes, modes and owners;
PID 1 must be launchd, mach.ko must be loaded, logd must be running, and a
unique logger record must be readable through log.
Failure leaves the update pending, records the reason in history and the log,
and makes status advise rollback.
For disposable testing only, a root-owned file not writable by group or other,
/var/db/softwareupdate/confirm-delay, may reduce the delay to 0 through 600
seconds. No such override is installed by default.
.Pp
.Cm confirm
requires the running environment to match pending-target and the previous
environment to exist. It makes the running environment persistent and clears
pending-target. When installation changed /boot/loader.efi, its manifest
identity is checked and the loader on the unique EFI system partition is
replaced atomically, at efi/freebsd/loader.efi or EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI.
Failure to locate or update the loader prevents confirmation.
Before activation, legacy snapshots named
.Cm @sealed
and held with the softwareupdate tag are bound to their owning environments
by GUID and renamed. This includes a pending environment staged by an older
installer. The predecessor's identity remains on its dataset when promotion
moves its snapshot, so rollback and verification can still locate that seal.
If activation fails after changing the pool's bootfs, confirmation restores
and checks the saved previous boot default without attempting another promote.
The update remains pending.
Manual and automatic confirmation failures record the error and command stderr
in history, the softwareupdate log and boot-check-failed, and print the reason.
Failure history rows append a reason field to the usual seven fields.
.Pp
.Cm cancel
before reboot clears the one-time selection and destroys the verified owned
clone, releasing its softwareupdate hold first.
On an unconfirmed staged boot it selects the previous environment.
.Cm rollback
selects previous-be persistently; with --restart it reboots after selection.
These commands never destroy the running environment.
.Pp
After confirmation the current environment and three other managed
environments are kept, always including the previous environment.
Older environments are removed only if an installation registry token and
the clone's creation marker agree. Foreign environments and environments
selected for a boot are never removed.
Lifecycle operations take an exclusive operation lock.
.Pp
Retention releases the softwareupdate hold immediately before destroying an
owned environment, locating and removing its seal by GUID even when promotion
has moved the snapshot to another dataset. Other ZFS holds still prevent
destruction. Rollback is
non-destructive and keeps its seal. Confirmation preserves the current and
previous environments' seals.
.Sh SEAL VERIFICATION
.Cm verify Op Fl -quiet
requires root and compares the running boot environment with its
.Cm @sealed-GUID
snapshot. Snapshot lookup uses the recorded GUID across the ROOT lineage,
including snapshots moved into another environment by promotion.
Legacy held snapshots named @sealed are bound before the next confirmation.
Status names the seal and reports its creation time as UTC Unix
seconds in seal_taken_unix.
An environment installed before sealing was introduced has no seal.
.Pp
The snapshot's installed manifest defines the base files; its directories.tsv
defines the base directories. Regular files are exhaustively compared by
SHA-256, type, mode and ownership with their sealed versions. The sealed
version is authoritative for generated files and existing directories that
were preserved at installation. ZFS diff supplies underlying-dataset directory
candidates, so devfs or tmpfs mounts do not appear as base modifications;
directory type, mode and ownership are then checked against the snapshot.
Runtime file additions outside the declared base are not reported.
.Pp
Changed paths are listed as modified, missing or type-changed. Ordinary owner
configuration files recorded with .rmxos-new sidecars at sealing are listed
separately as expected-owner-change, and do not count as drift when edited.
Missing files or changed types still count as drift. System configuration
under /etc/defaults and /etc/mtree is never exempted.
.Fl -quiet
prints only the numeric drift count when a seal is present.
.Pp
This seal is held, but the running filesystem is not read-only. It reports
drift; it does not prevent writes or authenticate the published update.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
For verify, 0 means the base matches, 1 means base drift, 2 means an operational
error or root refusal, and 3 means this boot environment has no seal.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sha256 1 ,
.Xr bectl 8
